1. Setting Up LineageOS and First Impressions

Once LineageOS is up and running, you'll be greeted by its initial setup process. At some point, it may offer to install Lineage Recovery. 
For this setup, stick with TWRP if you already have a compatible version installed and working. Recovery compatibility depends on your device and partition layout, so don't replace a working recovery without checking first.

After completing the initial setup, enable Developer Options and turn on USB debugging. We'll need it for some of the tools and modifications covered later in this guide.

2. Getting Magisk Ready

If Magisk is already installed, open it and check whether it needs any additional setup. Depending on how you rooted your device, you may see a prompt to complete the installation. 
Follow the instructions displayed by Magisk and reboot if required.

Once Magisk is working, open its settings and enable Zygisk if your setup supports it. Grant superuser access only to apps and tools that actually need it. 
Root is powerful, so don't hand out permissions blindly.

3. Installing LSPosed

LSPosed provides a framework for running modules that modify certain aspects of Android. It is optional unless a later step in this guide specifically depends on it.

Before installing anything, check that the LSPosed release is compatible with your Android version, Magisk installation, and device architecture. 
Download the appropriate module from its official project source, install it through Magisk's Modules section, and reboot if instructed.

Once the device starts again, check that LSPosed is active before proceeding. If it doesn't show up or fails to initialize, 
troubleshoot that first instead of stacking more modifications on top of a broken setup.

4. A Quite Look at the Visual Side

Now we can finally get to the fun part.

Before installing launchers, icon packs, and other customization tools, take a look at the appearance options already available in your system. 
If your build supports accent-color customization, try a shade of blue as a starting point.

Don't aim for minimalism just because it's the default look of many modern Android interfaces. 
We're going for the character of classic Android, not another generic home screen with a wallpaper and a row of rounded icons.

Need help picking a palette? is a useful place to experiment with complementary colors, shades, and accents. Choose a main color first, then build the rest of the interface around it. 
The goal isn't to make every element cyan; it's to make the colors feel like they belong together.

We'll get to the launcher itself next. For now, think of this as choosing the visual direction before we start moving things around.
